wm-darwin: write the firmware framebuffer (SFGOP) with aligned 32-bit stores only. The damage path
copied each row segment with memcpy, whose 16-byte and unaligned vector stores fault on device
memory: in QEMU (IOGOPFramebuffer on ramfb) the compositor died with SIGBUS as soon as a damaged
rectangle started at an x that is not a multiple of four pixels (typing in a terminal does that).
A core dump showed `stp q0, q1` at an address ending in 0x...b04 inside the framebuffer mapping,
from Scanout::write_damage. snapshot() and restore() read and write the mapping the same way.
The full-frame path already stored one word at a time.

diff -ru a/crates/wm-darwin/src/presenter.rs b/crates/wm-darwin/src/presenter.rs
--- a/crates/wm-darwin/src/presenter.rs	2026-11-18 04:11:41.520295622 -0800
+++ b/crates/wm-darwin/src/presenter.rs	2026-11-18 04:11:41.545648147 -0800
@@ -41,6 +41,23 @@
     /// Reused conversion buffer: up to `BAND_ROWS` full rows.
     band: Vec<u8>,
 }
+/// Copy `src` into the mapping at `dst` one aligned 32-bit store at a time. The mapping can be
+/// device memory (the firmware framebuffer, IOGOP on QEMU's ramfb), where the 16-byte and
+/// unaligned vector stores of memcpy fault: SIGBUS when a damage rectangle starts at an x that
+/// is not a multiple of four pixels. `dst` is 4-byte aligned (base, stride and x0 * 4 are).
+unsafe fn store_words(dst: *mut u8, src: &[u8]) {
+    let dst = dst as *mut u32;
+    for (i, w) in src.chunks_exact(4).enumerate() {
+        dst.add(i).write_volatile(u32::from_ne_bytes([w[0], w[1], w[2], w[3]]));
+    }
+}
+/// The reverse of `store_words`: aligned 32-bit loads from the mapping.
+unsafe fn load_words(src: *const u8, dst: &mut [u8]) {
+    let src = src as *const u32;
+    for (i, w) in dst.chunks_exact_mut(4).enumerate() {
+        w.copy_from_slice(&src.add(i).read_volatile().to_ne_bytes());
+    }
+}
 /// Rows converted before they are copied out. Small enough to stay in cache,
 /// large enough that the two clock readings per band cost nothing.
 const BAND_ROWS: usize = 16;
